tic_preflight","test":"uv run pytest"},"context":{"enabled":true,"entry_max_bytes":4000,"extra_paths":[],"max_bytes":24000},"diff":{"exclude":["*.lock","*-lock.json","vendor/**","**/*.min.js","**/*.min.css","**/__snapshots__/**","**/*.pb.go","**/*_pb2.py"],"max_bytes":500000},"docs":{"blocking_severities":["critical","high"],"enabled":true,"paths":[],"require_changelog":false},"gate":{"mode":"token"},"general":{"base_ref":"main"},"hook":{"allow_force_push":true},"policy":{"high_risk_paths":[],"human_review_paths":[".agentic-preflight.toml",".github/CODEOWNERS",".github/dependabot.yml",".github/workflows/**",".github/scripts/verify_dependabot.py","SECURITY.md","docs/examples/reviewers/**","agentic_preflight/approval.py","agentic_preflight/attestation.py","agentic_preflight/cli_policy.py","agentic_preflight/hook.py","agentic_preflight/risk.py","agentic_preflight/runs/publish.py"],"medium_risk_paths":["agentic_preflight/**","tests/**","skill/**"]},"pr":{"automated_cleanup":true,"mode":"auto"},"review":{"blocking_severities":["critical","high"],"command":"env AP_REVIEWER_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5 AP_REVIEWER_EFFORT=low uv run python -B docs/examples/reviewers/claude_review.py","executor":"command","max_findings":50,"require_command_for":[]},"stage":{"max_attempts":5,"max_restarts":5,"timeout_seconds":1500},"worktree":{"copy_files":[".env"],"mode":"in_place","root":null,"setup_command":null}},"findings":[{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"`commandmod.split` returns the quoted argument of `sh -c \"python3 tools/review.py\"` as a single word. That word is split only on shell operators, not on whitespace, so `_command_path` gets `python3 tools/review.py`. Its parent is `python3 tools`, so the pattern becomes `python3 tools/**`, which matches no real path. A branch can then rewrite `tools/review.py` with no human review, which is the bypass this advisory is meant to close.","fix_commit":"66867312be6ab721ecceb830c6f1db783cc14e59","id":"F001","line":101,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":null,"severity":"medium","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"Also split each word on whitespace before testing it as a path, or re-split the arguments of `-c` with the shell-word helper. Add a test such as `sh -c 'python3 tools/review.py'` -> `['tools/**']`.","title":"A reviewer script inside `sh -c`/`bash -c` quoting is mapped to a wrong pattern and stays unprotected","unit":"U0010"},{"action":"ask_user","code_owned":false,"detail":"For `cd tools&&python3 review.py` the code protects `review.py` and `review.py/**` at the root. The script that runs is `tools/review.py`, and it stays unprotected. A branch can rewrite it without human review, even though the command names it directly. The new test in tests/test_risk.py asserts this wrong result, and the docs do not mention `cd` as a limitation.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F002","line":101,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":"Fixed in 6686731 (same repair commit as F001): words after cd/pushd DIR are resolved against both the repository root and DIR, so 'cd tools&&python3 review.py' protects tools/**. Chose the fail-closed resolve-both reading over treating cd as unresolvable; documented in docs/configuration.md.","severity":"medium","stage":"review","status":"dismissed","suggestion":"Choose one: track a leading `cd <dir>` and resolve later relative words against that directory, or treat any command that uses `cd` as unresolvable and document that `trusted_paths` is required. Then fix the test's expected patterns to match.","title":"Paths after `cd` are resolved from the repository root, so the script the reviewer actually runs is not protected","unit":"U0010"},{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"The test expects `(\"cd tools&&python3 review.py\", [\"review.py\", \"review.py/**\"])`. That is a root path, not `tools/review.py`, the file the shell runs. The test pins the gap described above as intended behavior.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F003","line":177,"path":"tests/test_risk.py","response_note":"Fixed in 6686731 (same repair commit as F001): the test now expects tools/** for 'cd tools&&python3 review.py' and adds pushd, sh -c and bash -lc cases.","severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"dismissed","suggestion":"Expect `tools/**`, or remove the case and document `cd` as unsupported.","title":"Test locks in the wrong pattern for a command that starts with `cd`","unit":"U0031"},{"action":"ask_user","code_owned":false,"detail":"command_path_patterns() handles only the `cd` and `pushd` builtins when it changes the directory that later words resolve against. Some option forms also change the working directory: `uv run --directory tools python review.py`, `git -C tools ...`, `npm --prefix tools ...` and `make -C tools`. In these, the directory is a separate word with no `/` or `.`, so _looks_like_path skips it. `review.py` then resolves only at the repository root, which gives the patterns `review.py` and `review.py/**`. A branch can rewrite `tools/review.py`, which the reviewer actually runs, and the change stays low risk. That is the bypass GHSA-3372-q2xp-jr7q describes. The docs list cd/pushd and tell users to add other cases to `trusted_paths`. The gap is still silent for a common `uv run --directory` form. Someone needs to decide whether to recognise these options or warn about them.","fix_commit":"4429f622d8da29edae83494ebd3b8e8c2acd82e7","id":"F004","line":136,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":null,"severity":"medium","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"Treat the value of known directory options (`--directory`, `--cwd`, `--prefix`, `-C`, in both the `--opt DIR` and `--opt=DIR` forms) like a `cd` target. Alternatively, name this case explicitly in docs/configuration.md and add a test that pins the chosen behavior.","title":"Directory-changing options other than cd/pushd leave the real reviewer script unprotected","unit":"U0010"},{"action":"ask_user","code_owned":false,"detail":"command_path_patterns protects a root-level script only as `rev.py` and `rev.py/**`. The docstring gives the reason for protecting the parent directory: interpreters load sibling modules from the script's directory. That reason holds at the root too. A command such as `python3 review.py`, where review.py imports a root-level `review_common.py`, therefore leaves the imported module unprotected. A branch could rewrite that module without needing human review. The docs describe the root-file behaviour, but they do not mention this gap.","fix_commit":"641ba1960d9e2c76e75fbb8ba8e6a3ee4b9c9015","id":"F005","line":170,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":null,"severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"Say in docs/configuration.md and docs/limits.md that modules imported by a root-level reviewer must be listed in `trusted_paths`. Alternatively, protect the root-level `*.py` files next to the script.","title":"A reviewer script at the repository root does not protect the modules beside it","unit":"U0010"},{"action":"no_op","code_owned":false,"detail":"_looks_like_path accepts any word that contains a dot. Words like `--python=3.12` or `AP_REVIEWER_MODEL=gpt-6.1-sol` produce root patterns such as `3.12` and `3.12/**`. The extra patterns are harmless because they fail closed, but they could appear in path_reasons if a file with that name is ever changed.","fix_commit":null,"id":"F006","line":135,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":"Extra root patterns from dotted non-path words (versions, model ids) fail closed and only appear if a root file with that exact name changes; filtering them would risk missing real script names.","severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"accepted","suggestion":null,"title":"Dotted non-path words such as versions or model IDs become human-review patterns","unit":"U0010"}],"fingerprint":{"base_tree_sha":"725a2d01be4301c7430140c65460bee2beec119e","config_sha256":"13dc0fe89439d5a0ae1e40554eae1198